nd Society , vol. ii (2nd edn, London, 1992). Patrick Riley’s(@)(@)(@@) Will and(@)Political Legitimacy: A Critical Exposition of Social Contract Theory in Hobbes, Locke, Rousseau, Kant and Hegel (Cambridge, Mass., 1982) offers an especially salient treatment of Rousseau’s conception of the general will as part of a tradition of political voluntarism, while Richard Fralin’s(@)(@)(@@) Rousseau and Representation (New York, 1978) attempts to bring the 
